/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_Install Install
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Delivery
 * @brief Install an already configured build into a selected prefix.
 *
 * Install does not configure or build the project and does not delete existing
 * content. It always installs Release, which must already be configured and
 * compiled. Use install when the caller controls the destination and wants
 * normal CMake overlay semantics.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowInstallBash Bash
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/install.sh <preset> [--prefix <path>]
 *
 * ./scripts/install.sh macos_arm64 --prefix /opt/Foundation
 * ./scripts/install.sh macos_arm64
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowInstallPowerShell PowerShell
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/install.ps1 <preset> [-Prefix <path>]
 * @endcode
 *
 * Without an explicit prefix, the destination is `dist/<preset>`. The installed
 * tree contains public headers, the static library, and the CMake package files
 * required by `find_package(Foundation CONFIG)`. The installed package contains
 * only the Release library and `FoundationTargets-release.cmake`.
 *
 * Debug remains a source-tree build configuration for development, diagnostics,
 * and tests. It is intentionally not installed. Consumers that must step into a
 * Debug Foundation build should build Foundation from source rather than expect
 * a precompiled Debug archive in the distribution package.
 *
 * Install never removes stale files. If a feature was disabled after an earlier
 * installation, old headers or archives may remain in the prefix. Use export for
 * a clean repository-owned destination, or clean a caller-owned prefix yourself.
 *
 * Use @ref Foundation_Workflows_Export when a clean, reproducible distribution
 * should be created in one command.
 */
